WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.999
Even if you don’t know her by name, you
have most likely seen Frida Kahlo’s paintings

00:00:03.999 --> 00:00:04.999
before.

00:00:04.999 --> 00:00:09.450
She is the Mexican woman with a bold unibrow,
wearing a flower crown and brightly colored

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:10.450
clothing.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:14.950
Kahlo was physically disabled her entire adult
life, and yet she loved herself enough to

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:16.300
be her own muse.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.910
Years before her time, she challenged society’s
expectations, and was able to create Surrealist

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.960
works of art that examined the inner workings
of the human soul.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.680
On today’s Biographics, we will tell you
the story of the great artist, Frida Kahlo.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:44.710
The Birth of an Artist
Magdalena Carmen Frieda Kahlo y Calderón

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:48.840
was born on July 6th, 1907 in Coyoacan, Mexico.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.780
Her family lived in a blue house appropriately
named; La Casa Azul.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.210
Her father was a German photographer, and
her mother was Spanish and Native American.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:01.350
Frida’s father used her as a model in his
photography studio, and it was during this

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.510
time that she learned how to be photogenic.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.390
Even from an early age, she had an incredibly
intense gaze into the camera lens.

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:12.660
By the time she was 17, she was fed up with
wearing all of the frilly dresses that her

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.690
father put her in, and she chose to pose in
a man’s suit during one of their family

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:20.810
photos, which, of course, was completely frowned
upon in the 1920’s.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:25.299
As a child, she suffered from Polio, and one
leg was permanently shorter than the other.

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:27.960
But she never let that stop her from being
adventurous.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.280
She still climbed trees, danced, and played
outside with her friends.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.590
In 1922, she began attending the National
Preparatory School in Mexico City with the

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.840
intention of becoming a doctor.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.020
But when she was 18 years old, she was on
a bus heading to school, when it collided

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.240
with a tram car.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.780
During the crash, a metal handrail impaled
her body, going straight through her torso

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.970
to the point where the rod was sticking out
the other end.

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:53.460
She had also fractured her spine in multiple
places, as well as a broken pelvis, hips,

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.700
collarbone, both of her legs, and a few ribs.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.759
She had lost so much blood, the doctors did
not think she would make it.

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:04.729
By no small miracle, Frida Kahlo miraculously
survived the tram accident.

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:07.700
Frida laid in a hospital bed while her entire
body healed.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.060
The doctor told her that because of where
the metal rod had impaled her, it was unlikely

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.310
that she would ever be able to get pregnant.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:18.700
Even after several operations, she was now
disabled, and the injuries would still cause

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.670
her paint for the rest of her life.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.830
Her boyfriend, Alejandro Gómez Arias, had
been on the bus as well, but he survived the

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.430
crash with only minor injuries.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:30.420
When she wrote letters to him, she often asked
questions like, “What will my life be like

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.849
30 years from now?” and she knew that the
rest of her existence would be an uphill battle.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:40.691
As you can imagine, her entire body had to
be put into casts, and she was not allowed

00:02:40.691 --> 00:02:43.260
to leave her hospital bed for several months.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.240
During this time when she was bedridden, her
father helped to set up a specially made easel

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.550
to hang over her bed, so that she could spend
her days painting.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.240
Since she could not go outside, everything
Frida painted was from her memory.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.460
She was painting her own Surrealist versions
of reality, along with symbols of Mexican

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.390
religion and culture.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:06.080
Frida also painted and sketched herself, in
order to express how her pain felt through

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.080
drawings.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:11.650
There is no doubt that even though her body
had been weakened, her mind was still incredibly

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.030
strong, and that helped keep her going.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.519
Frida once wrote, “Feet, what do I need
you for, when I have wings to fly?”

00:03:18.519 --> 00:03:23.700
As time went on, she chose to no longer groom
herself the way women were expected to.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.390
She was trying to heal from the accident,
and there was no time to tweeze her eyebrows

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.970
or shave her upper lip.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:33.920
When she painted “Self Portrait in a Velvet
Dress”, she kept all of those details true

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.170
to real life, even though she could have easily
painted out what society deemed as imperfections.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.590
This level of honesty was incredibly brave,
even by today’s standards, when you think

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.190
about how many selfies are taken with Snapchat
filters and Facetune.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:53.049
But at this point, Frida Kahlo was just happy
to be alive, and began to embrace the body

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:54.379
she was born with.

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:57.599
Once she left the hospital, Frida returned
to school, but she was now in a wheelchair.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:01.880
While her friends were still spending time
with her, they wanted to continue living their

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.760
young, vibrant lives, and they drifted in
different directions.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.140
She and Alejandro Gómez Arias broke up, and
she decided to focus on the life she could

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.020
cultivate, which was her mind, and her art.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:17.509
So she join her University’s chapter of
the Mexican Communist Party, and it would

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:24.219
set her on course for the rest of her life.

00:04:24.219 --> 00:04:29.229
Love
In order to understand the full story of Frida

00:04:29.229 --> 00:04:33.089
Kahlo’s epic love, we need to go back to
when she was 15 years old.

00:04:33.089 --> 00:04:38.309
A famous artist named Diego Rivera was painting
a mural in the Simon Bolivar Amphitheater

00:04:38.309 --> 00:04:40.219
at the National Preparatory School.

00:04:40.219 --> 00:04:41.589
Frida would sneak in to watch him.

00:04:41.589 --> 00:04:47.330
She was fascinated by his work, and he completely
inspired her to become a great artist.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:52.490
Like most teenagers with a crush, she swooned
to her best friend about how great he was,

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.650
and say that one day, she would marry him
and have his babies.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:59.919
But then, when he left the painting to take
a break, she put soap all over his ladder,

00:04:59.919 --> 00:05:01.919
and teased him when he slipped and fell.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:06.819
This was enough for him to notice her, but
not enough to reciprocate her feelings, of

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:07.819
course.

00:05:07.819 --> 00:05:11.490
After all, he was 35 at the time, and from
his perspective, this was just a kid who was

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:12.800
trying to distract him.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.120
But he couldn’t help but notice that she
“had an unusual dignity and self-assurance,

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:20.879
and there was a strange fire in her eyes.”

00:05:20.879 --> 00:05:23.469
Fast forward to 1928.

00:05:23.469 --> 00:05:26.830
Frida was 21 years old, and it had been 3
years since her accident.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:31.001
Through an intense amount of physical therapy,
she finally learned to walk again, but it

00:05:31.001 --> 00:05:35.699
was still necessary for her to wear back braces
and corsets made of plaster, in order to keep

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:37.240
her spine straight.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.659
Sometimes, the pain was still too much to
bear, and she would have to use a wheelchair.

00:05:41.659 --> 00:05:46.080
She was all grown up now, and in the 6 years
since she last saw Rivera, she had become

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.460
a beautiful young woman.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.009
Even though it had been years, she would still
visit his famous murals around the city, and

00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:54.300
followed the progress of his artistic career.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:58.229
She heard that he was painting another mural
at Mexico City’s Ministry of Education,

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:00.149
so she began to show up every day.

00:06:00.149 --> 00:06:04.819
This time, she sat quietly and respectfully,
watching and studying him as he painted.

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:09.499
One day, Frida Kahlo bravely approached Diego
Rivera, and told him that she had admired

00:06:09.499 --> 00:06:10.659
his talent for years.

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:15.080
She explained that she was an aspiring artist,
and wanted to know his opinion of her work.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:19.059
At first, he did not recognize her, but once
she explained the story of the soap on the

00:06:19.059 --> 00:06:23.619
ladder, he instantly made the connection,
because she still had that same fire in her

00:06:23.619 --> 00:06:24.619
eyes.

00:06:24.619 --> 00:06:28.759
Frida invited him over to La Casa Azul, where
he could see all of her work in her studio.

00:06:28.759 --> 00:06:32.689
After seeing Frida’s art, he told her, “In
my opinion, no matter how difficult it might

00:06:32.689 --> 00:06:35.300
be for you, you must continue to paint.”

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.830
While he was in her home, he could see how
much joy and passion she had for painting,

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:42.409
and it rejuvenated his love for his own work.

00:06:42.409 --> 00:06:47.679
He said, “I did not know it then, but Frida
had already become the most important fact

00:06:47.679 --> 00:06:48.740
of my life.”

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.559
They began to spend more time together, and
went on several dates.

00:06:51.559 --> 00:06:54.129
A few days later, he kissed her for the first
time.

00:06:54.129 --> 00:06:58.770
This was just the beginning of a relationship
that would last the rest of her life.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:03.330
Diego Rivera and Frida Kahlo were married
in 1929, and they continued to be one another’s

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:04.330
muses.

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.619
Their relationship inspired one another to
keep creating more and more amazing works.

00:07:08.619 --> 00:07:11.929
In one of her letters to him, she wrote to
Diego, “I’d like to paint you, but there

00:07:11.929 --> 00:07:17.249
are no colors, because there are so many,
in my confusion, the tangible form of my great

00:07:17.249 --> 00:07:18.249
love.”

00:07:18.249 --> 00:07:26.380
Career &amp; Communism
Frida and Diego were both part of a group

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:30.819
of artists and intellectuals who would later
be labeled as part of the “Mexicanidad”

00:07:30.819 --> 00:07:34.159
movement, which was a re-invigorated sense
of national pride.

00:07:34.159 --> 00:07:39.020
Instead of trying to copy European artists,
they wanted to create a new art style that

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:40.289
identified Mexican culture.

00:07:40.289 --> 00:07:43.990
Aside from their mutual love of art, Frida
and Diego also discussed politics at great

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:44.990
length.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:48.529
Diego was so-well known, that he became a
political activist in the Mexican Communist

00:07:48.529 --> 00:07:52.720
Party, and was often painting Soviet symbolism
into his works of art.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:56.469
Diego Rivera was already wealthy from the
sales of his art, and as the years went on,

00:07:56.469 --> 00:08:01.559
the rest of the world was beginning to respect
and appreciate his Mexicanidad style, as well.

00:08:01.559 --> 00:08:04.639
He was invited to paint several murals across
the United States.

00:08:04.639 --> 00:08:09.309
At the time, no one was paying much attention
to Frida Kahlo’s work, because she was just

00:08:09.309 --> 00:08:11.680
the wife of this far more famous artist.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.009
In 1931, they were living in New York City,
and she was miserable with the vibe of the

00:08:16.009 --> 00:08:17.009
town.

00:08:17.009 --> 00:08:19.330
In a letter to a friend, she wrote, “I'm
not painting or doing anything.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:24.550
I dislike the ‘high society’ here, and
feel a little rage against all these fat cats,

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:27.709
since I've seen thousands of people in terrible
misery.”

00:08:27.709 --> 00:08:32.220
For years, Rivera was slipping Communist iconography
into his murals across the United States,

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:33.750
but they mostly went unnoticed.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:39.349
In 1933, Diego Rivera painted a mural that
was meant to go in the lobby of the Rockefeller

00:08:39.349 --> 00:08:41.449
Center, called “Man at the Crossroads”.

00:08:41.449 --> 00:08:44.290
But he clearly included the image of Lenin.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:49.610
Once they spotted this, the officials demanded
that he removed all Communist imagery, but

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:52.240
he refused to do so, and the painting remained
unfinished.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.079
After spending several years traveling, and
Diego had an architect build them a two seperate

00:08:56.079 --> 00:09:00.629
houses that were side-by-side and connected
by a bridge, so that they could live together,

00:09:00.629 --> 00:09:03.339
and still have each of their own private art
studios.

00:09:03.339 --> 00:09:07.680
Eventually, the couple returned to Frida’s
childhood home La Casa Azul, which she inherited

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:08.680
from her parents.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:13.720
In 1937, they met the Communist revolutionary,
Leon Trotsky, who was living in exile.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:18.139
Since they were already huge supporters of
the USSR, they invited him to live with them

00:09:18.139 --> 00:09:19.410
in La Casa Azul.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.470
For years, there was an ongoing rumor that
Frida Kahlo and Leon Trotsky were having an

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:24.470
affair.

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:28.460
She even gave him one of her self-portraits,
with a note that said, "To Leon Trotsky, with

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:31.020
all my love, I dedicate this painting.”

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:35.750
The rumor about their affair was published
over and over again so many times, it was

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:37.459
eventually taken as historic fact.

00:09:37.459 --> 00:09:42.100
It was even included in the plot of the 2002
movie Frida, starring Salma Hayek.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:46.560
However, it was later discovered that the
affair may have never taken place, after all.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.790
In his last will and testament, Diego Rivera
strictly forbid any of his personal paperwork

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:54.709
to be released to the public until 50 years
after his dead.

00:09:54.709 --> 00:09:58.589
He ordered that all of their private letters
and documents should be hidden inside of their

00:09:58.589 --> 00:10:01.029
bathroom wall at La Casa Azul.

00:10:01.029 --> 00:10:05.579
So, when they were unveiled in 2007, the public
finally got to read the letters between Kahlo

00:10:05.579 --> 00:10:06.579
and Trotsky.

00:10:06.579 --> 00:10:09.760
While it’s true that they frequently wrote
to one another and spent a great deal of time

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:14.329
together in their home, these hidden letters
reveal that the correspondence between Kahlo

00:10:14.329 --> 00:10:16.350
and Trotsky was strictly platonic.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:20.959
In fact, Frida Kahlo spent most of her letters
supporting her husband, trying to convince

00:10:20.959 --> 00:10:25.690
Trotsky that he deserved to have a more prominent
role in the Communist party.

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:29.910
She even called Trotsky out on his behavior
as being egotistical, and revealed that she

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:31.050
did not like him at all.

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:35.899
She once wrote, “ I observed that whenever
there was a discussion of any kind, and Diego

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:41.080
gave his opinion, you always took it with
a certain irony and doubtfulness of its truthfulness.

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:47.660
This kind of irony in time gets on one's nerves”
Finding Herself

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:54.130
Throughout her life, Frida had to undergo
a total of 30 different operations.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:58.230
One of her most famous paintings, “The Broken
Column”, depicts how she truly felt inside.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:01.120
Since she was constantly in and out of the
hospital, she became very good friends with

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.120
her doctor.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:06.570
There were a series of letters that revealed
that she became pregnant with Diego Rivera’s

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:07.570
child.

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:10.040
This came as a shock to her, since she had
been told that she would never be able to

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:11.040
have children.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.870
However, her doctor urged her to get an abortion,
because he explained that her body would not

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:18.540
be able to take the pregnancy to full term.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:22.250
However, the guilt of the abortion was overwhelming.

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:26.170
When she got pregnant a second time in 1932,
she tried to keep the baby, even if it meant

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:29.240
breaking bones and sacrificing her own body.

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:33.970
But the scarring in her uterus was too extensive
to bring the pregnancy to term, and Frida

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:35.350
suffered a miscarriage.

00:11:35.350 --> 00:11:38.959
On one of her body corsets, Frida painted
the Communist symbol over her heart, and an

00:11:38.959 --> 00:11:43.529
image of a fetus over her belly, to symbolize
the two unborn children she had lost.

00:11:43.529 --> 00:11:46.670
She wore it like a medal of honor underneath
her clothes.

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:50.959
Since she was disabled and in near-constant
pain, Frida could not fully satisfy Diego

00:11:50.959 --> 00:11:52.029
Rivera’s libido.

00:11:52.029 --> 00:11:55.689
He was also a celebrity, so there was no shortage
of female attention.

00:11:55.689 --> 00:11:59.970
Frida was fully aware of his infidelity, but
she had also become totally dependent on his

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:03.060
care, and addicted to his love and affection.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:08.139
Instead of divorcing him, she took the opportunity
to have an affair with the photographer Nickolas

00:12:08.139 --> 00:12:12.570
Muray, and she also experiment with her sexuality
by having affairs with several women.

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:18.160
She became good friends with the actress Josephine
Baker, and the artist Georgia O'Keeffe, who

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:22.529
was famous for making paintings of flowers
that actually look like- well...you know what

00:12:22.529 --> 00:12:23.529
they look like.

00:12:23.529 --> 00:12:27.310
Frida and Diego would often throw parties
at their home, and the famous Mexican musician

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:29.990
Chavela Vargas was invited to one of them.

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:32.319
After seeing Frida for the first time, Vargas
said,

00:12:32.319 --> 00:12:33.839
“She was like a vision.

00:12:33.839 --> 00:12:37.680
When I saw her face, her eyes- I thought she
was from another world.”

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:41.889
The two become very close, and had an affair
that lasted much longer than the other flings

00:12:41.889 --> 00:12:43.069
she had with women.

00:12:43.069 --> 00:12:47.730
During an interview, Chavela Vargas expressed
that she truly did love Frida Kahlo, and she

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:50.170
drew all of her artistic inspiration from
her.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:54.810
Frida’s favorite song of hers was called
“La Llarona”, which is based on the terrifying

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.889
Mexican legend of The Weeping Woman.

00:12:56.889 --> 00:13:01.100
However, Frida Kahlo was still in love with
Diego, and she was never going to leave her

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:02.410
husband for another woman.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:06.490
This relationship of unrequited love was breaking
young Vargas’ heart.

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:09.459
She wrote the song “Paloma Negra”, or
“Black Dove”.

00:13:09.459 --> 00:13:13.199
In it, you can clearly hear the anguish in
her voice.

00:13:13.199 --> 00:13:17.470
Even though she claimed to be fine with their
mutual unfaithfulness, Diego Rivera crossed

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:19.209
the line when he slept with her sister.

00:13:19.209 --> 00:13:20.209
Yikes.

00:13:20.209 --> 00:13:21.680
They got a divorce in the year 1940.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.399
One month later, she cut off all her hair,
and painted a self-portrait dressed in one

00:13:25.399 --> 00:13:26.399
of Diego’s suits.

00:13:26.399 --> 00:13:30.389
At the top of the painting, she included a
bar of musical notes, and the lyrics of a

00:13:30.389 --> 00:13:33.819
famous Mexican folk song about falling out
of love.

00:13:33.819 --> 00:13:38.389
Unfortunately, though, love is an incredibly
complex emotion, and it doesn’t just turn

00:13:38.389 --> 00:13:39.389
itself off overnight.

00:13:39.389 --> 00:13:43.490
She expressed her inner turmoil with a double
self-portrait called The Two Fridas, showing

00:13:43.490 --> 00:13:47.990
her past self on the left, which was wearing
a white dress, symbolizing her past purity

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:48.990
and innocence.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:52.660
The young Frida had her heart ripped out of
her chest, and she gave it to the Frida on

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:55.379
the right, who was older, and much stronger.

00:13:55.379 --> 00:13:59.889
But she still held onto the memory of Diego,
by holding his portrait in her hands.

00:13:59.889 --> 00:14:04.379
Even though she was clearly in a toxic relationship,
Frida and Diego felt that they could not live

00:14:04.379 --> 00:14:08.779
without one another, and they got remarried
less than a year after their divorce.

00:14:08.779 --> 00:14:13.259
There is a saying that great art comes from
pain, and in a way, this betrayal actually

00:14:13.259 --> 00:14:15.589
helped to further along her solo career.

00:14:15.589 --> 00:14:19.610
In 1947, the “Two Fridas” painting was
purchased by the National Institute of Fine

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:23.440
Art in Mexico City for 4,000 pesos, which
is roughly $1,000.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:28.250
After modern-day inflation, that’s closer
to $11,399.

00:14:28.250 --> 00:14:32.769
Finally, the art world was beginning to recognize
her talent, and she was no longer just the

00:14:32.769 --> 00:14:35.160
wife of Diego Rivera.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:43.230
Death, and Legacy
In 1953, Frida Kahlo finally had her very

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:44.709
first solo art exhibit.

00:14:44.709 --> 00:14:46.459
But her health was declining.

00:14:46.459 --> 00:14:51.189
She was so physically weak and in pain, her
doctor advised her to stay in bed.

00:14:51.189 --> 00:14:55.660
Even when she was sick, her soul was still
strong enough to demand that she must go to

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:56.660
her art show.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:01.269
That night, the patrons of the arts received
the grandest entrance they had ever seen,

00:15:01.269 --> 00:15:05.499
as Frida Kahlo’s four poster bed was carried
in on a stretcher by a group of men.

00:15:05.499 --> 00:15:10.630
She was all done up in a beautiful dress,
makeup, and a crown of flowers on her head.

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:14.509
Sadly, her illness got worse, and that same
year, she contracted gangrene in one of her

00:15:14.509 --> 00:15:16.579
legs, and it had to be amputated.

00:15:16.579 --> 00:15:20.970
Even so, she tried to stay strong, and decorated
her prosthetic leg to make it beautiful.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:24.680
But she was already in constant pain from
her earlier injuries.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.120
Losing her leg made it even more unbearable.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.399
She learned how to walk again, and even tried
to go dancing in her new leg.

00:15:30.399 --> 00:15:34.279
Her doctor prescribed her pain medication
to help dull her senses, but it was still

00:15:34.279 --> 00:15:36.720
a life of near-constant misery.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:39.910
On July 7th, 1954, she turned 47 years old.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:42.560
Her friends and family threw her a birthday
celebration.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.509
There were over 100 people there.

00:15:44.509 --> 00:15:48.519
For the first time in months, she dressed
in her finest clothes, put on makeup, and

00:15:48.519 --> 00:15:50.480
donned the flowers in her hair again.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:55.020
But once the party was over, and the guests
went home, she was left alone to face her

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:56.040
reality.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:59.959
Perhaps she knew that from then on, things
were only going to get worse.

00:15:59.959 --> 00:16:05.119
Her health was declining, and she struggled
to accomplish even the smallest daily task.

00:16:05.119 --> 00:16:09.149
Just a few days after her party, on July 12th,
Kahlo was lying on her childhood bedroom in

00:16:09.149 --> 00:16:10.149
La Casa Azul.

00:16:10.149 --> 00:16:12.389
She sketched an image of an angel with black
wings.

00:16:12.389 --> 00:16:18.019
In her journal, she wrote, “I hope the exit
is joyful- and I hope to never come back.

00:16:18.019 --> 00:16:19.019
-Frida.”

00:16:19.019 --> 00:16:21.410
The next morning on July 13th, Diego found
her.

00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:23.480
She died of a pulmonary embolism.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:27.889
Most speculate that this was caused by overdosing
on her pain medication, and that she chose

00:16:27.889 --> 00:16:28.889
to commit suicide.

00:16:28.889 --> 00:16:32.759
Her family visited the home, and helped to
prepare her body, before it was taken to the

00:16:32.759 --> 00:16:35.980
Palace of Fine Art for a public viewing and
funeral.

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:40.189
Diego Rivera was in such shock, he refused
to believe that she was truly dead.

00:16:40.189 --> 00:16:44.709
He got into an argument with her doctor, refusing
to bury her until he had substantial proof

00:16:44.709 --> 00:16:45.839
that she was truly gone.

00:16:45.839 --> 00:16:49.540
Afterwards, they draped her coffin in a black
cloth with red flowers.

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:53.129
Diego insisted on draping the Communist symbol
over her coffin, as well.

00:16:53.129 --> 00:16:58.189
Over the course of her lifetime, Frida Kahlo
created a total of 143 paintings, and 55 of

00:16:58.189 --> 00:16:59.189
them were self-portraits.

00:16:59.189 --> 00:17:02.889
She became the first Latin American woman
to have one of her paintings displayed in

00:17:02.889 --> 00:17:04.679
the Louvre Museum in Paris.

00:17:04.679 --> 00:17:09.559
She has become such a well-known artist, that
people simply refer to her as “Frida”.

00:17:09.559 --> 00:17:12.500
Even though she is gone, she is anything but
forgotten.

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.949
Today, her work has become more relevant than
ever.

00:17:14.949 --> 00:17:19.209
Her works and personal letters are considered
to be so valuable, that they sell for millions

00:17:19.209 --> 00:17:20.230
of dollars at auction.

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:24.280
And in recent years, there has been a strong
resurgence of appreciation for Frida Kahlo,

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:45.789
and she has truly cemented herself in history
as a true Mexican icon.

